  "body": "Hi,\r\nI feel like there should be a shortcut to link to HTML landmarks nowadays. Is there?\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nFor accessibility I want to add a couple skip links to satisfy WCAG Success Criterion 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks (Level A).  \r\n\r\nI do realize that a sufficient technique is simply having landmarks in the first place. For example i can use landmarks as a form of skip links in some assisting technologies. \r\n\r\nHowever if I am a keyboard user, I love having a skip to main content link at the 1st tab stop. I regularly implement those for work. \r\n\r\nI'm coding things like  this:\r\n\r\n`<a href=\"#main\"...>skip,,,</a>`\r\nAnd then I have to give some element id=\"main\" or name=\"main\" as an attribute for the anchor link.\r\n\r\nIncreasingly, a good target might be the `<main>` tag (right?)  So I have even seen like this: `<main role=\"main\" id=\"main\">` redundant and extra code to me.  \r\n\r\nI'd love either that a landmark inherently owns id=\"[itself]\", and not allow or recommend those as id names elsewhere....\r\n\r\nOr\r\nHow about some other character instead of # to anchor link directly to a landmarks tag? <a href=\"+main\" or whatever instead of the + or #.\r\n\r\nThoughts?\r\n\r\nCheers,\r\nTim Sartoris ",
